WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.520
IOL Optics Atlas.

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:06.580
A guided tour, in about seven minutes.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.870
Two lenses in the same class can look very different at night.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:18.380
An autorefractor can misread a perfectly good multifocal eye.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.050
And every degree a toric lens rotates costs part of its effect.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:28.380
How do you show all of this to a resident, or a patient, in one picture?

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.670
That is what this atlas is for.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:37.590
Open iolatlas.com in any browser: on a laptop, a tablet or a phone.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.750
It is free, and there is nothing to install.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:46.640
Once it has loaded, you can add it to your home screen, and it opens even offline.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:54.000
The welcome screen lets you search for a lens, a machine, a formula or a term.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.950
Or simply enter the atlas.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.110
Page zero is the map.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:11.390
Eighteen pages, in five sections that follow the surgical pathway: understand the lens, find a lens,
plan the eye, after surgery, and background and method.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:19.420
One optics engine draws every lens the same way,
so the differences between designs are like for like.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.360
Three habits make the atlas easy.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:29.500
Right-click, or press and hold on a phone, any lens, class or term for its details.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.580
Open How to use on any page.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:46.890
And press References in the page footer: every value is linked to its source, ranked in six tiers,
from regulatory filings and peer-reviewed papers down to the atlas's own estimates, which are always marked.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:49.630
Section one.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.710
Understand the lens.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:02:07.020
Page one sorts lenses by the 2026 global consensus of ESCRS, ASCRS,
APACRS and LATAMSCRS: six classes by depth of focus, from narrowed to continuous.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.560
The rows show the optical principle behind each one.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:17.410
Right-click a class to see its criteria and its mean defocus curve.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.470
Page two builds generic designs from first principles.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:28.130
Each defocus curve is computed by diffraction through the optic and an average cornea.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:36.150
Change the pupil, or the corneal spherical aberration after laser vision correction,
and see which designs depend on them.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:42.590
Page three runs thirty-eight commercial lenses through the same engine.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:49.400
The table gives acuity at distance,
at sixty-six and at forty centimetres, and the depth of focus.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:55.730
Best perform lays the best published clinical curve over the model,
so you can see the gap.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.160
Both eyes adds the binocular curve.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:13.260
The lens lab opens one optic: its profile, how the light divides between the foci as the pupil changes,
the focal volume, and a simulated letter chart at distance, intermediate and near.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:19.770
Compare two IOLs puts their specifications side by side, each value with its source tier.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:22.740
Section two.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.200
Find a lens.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.800
Trade-offs summarises bench and clinical patterns for each design: pick two, and compare.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:41.430
The Asia-Pacific and world directories list presbyopia-correcting lenses by depth-of-focus class, with toric availability.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:46.380
Registration differs by country, so always confirm local availability.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.400
Section three.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.800
Plan the eye.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:04.020
Page eight explains how each optical biometer measures, compares two devices row by row,
and traces each clearance to its FDA summary where one exists.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:10.490
Page nine sorts thirty-eight power formulas by generation and principle.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:16.910
Pick a formula, and the inputs it reads light up in turn,
then the lens position it predicts.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.570
Page ten connects the anterior segment to the toric effect.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:29.530
The cylinder on a toric lens is fixed,
but its effect at the cornea depends on where the lens sits.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.830
Move the effective lens position, and watch the ratio change.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:38.680
Posterior corneal astigmatism and your incision are entered here too.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:50.370
Page eleven shows what rotation costs: about three and a half percent of the cylinder for every degree, and the full original cylinder at thirty degrees.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.240
Section four.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:54.720
After surgery.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:02.430
Page twelve shows a distant point of light through each optic, at a night pupil.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:06.350
The defocused near and intermediate images form the rings.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:12.980
Their size grows with add power and pupil,
and their brightness follows the light in those orders.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:26.210
Page thirteen explains why autorefractors misread pseudophakic and multifocal eyes,
and shows the reading you can expect for each IOL, compared with manifest refraction.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:37.660
Page fourteen gives each eye its own refraction, aberration, pupil and lens,
then combines the two by binocular contrast summation.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.370
Section five.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.400
Background and method.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:56.740
The background pages show how an IOL is measured on the optical bench,
the Zernike terms up to the eighth order in three dimensions, and what combined aberrations do to a letter chart.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.480
Page eighteen describes the model, its sources and its limits.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:07.610
The atlas is a teaching model, not a tool for planning a patient's surgery.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:13.630
It compares lenses with each other, and it is checked against published clinical curves.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:23.060
For your lectures, press Present for sixteen-by-nine plates,
choose a white or a black background, and cite the atlas.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.280
Non-profit teaching and research use is welcome.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.800
IOL Optics Atlas.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.360
See how every IOL bends light.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.250
At iolatlas.com.
